Okay, assuming you use bash and have vi(m), type:

   vim .bashrc

If the file doesn't already exist, type i, then type:

   NETHACKOPTIONS="<what you want for your nethack options>"

Once you do that, hit ESC, then type:

   :wq

This saves the file; if you don't have a .bash_profile, bash
automatically reads .bashrc anyways [IIRC]. Alternatively, you could, at
the commandline [which might look like yoshi@gaia:~$, but most likely
isn't] type:

   NETHACKOPTIONS="<nethack options>"

This isn't permanent, however, which is why I went through the .bashrc
deal first.

If you're using csh or some other shell, I don't know what the proper
commands are, or how to configure the appropriate .*rc to make it
permanent. I'm not sure if 'env' works across shells. If it works, it'll
tell you what shell you're using.

